epictetus-1.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: epictetus-1.logic.r2 has nothing from it; epictetus-1.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-1.logic.r2.3 have "(divine_faithful → man_faithful) ∧ (divine_free → man_free) ∧ (divine_beneficent → man_beneficent) ∧ (divine_magnanimous → man_magnanimous)"; they differ when only "the divine is faithful", "the divine is free", "the divine is beneficent", "the divine is magnanimous", "man must be faithful", "man must be free" and "man must be beneficent" are true, which the first allows
epictetus-1.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps epictetus-1.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the divine is faithful", "the divine is free", "the divine is beneficent", "the divine is magnanimous", "man must be faithful", "man must be free" and "man must be beneficent" are true and "man must be magnanimous" is false. Is S6, "If the divine is faithful, man also must be faithful; if it is free, man also must be free; if beneficent, man also must be beneficent; if magnanimous, man also must be magnanimous; as being then an imitator of God he must do and say everything consistently with this fact.", true then?
epictetus-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: epictetus-1.logic.r2 has "carpenter_learns ∧ pilot_learns ∧ philosophy_like_crafts → must_learn ∧ ¬wish_sufficient" and "philosophy_like_crafts" and "must_be_like → (divine_faithful → man_faithful) ∧ (divine_free → man_free) ∧ (divine_beneficent → man_beneficent) ∧ (divine_magnanimous → man_magnanimous)"; epictetus-1.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-1.logic.r2.3 have "philosophy_like_crafts" and "carpenter_learns ∧ pilot_learns ∧ philosophy_like_crafts → ¬wish_sufficient ∧ must_learn"; they differ when only "the carpenter becomes a carpenter when he has learned certain things", "the pilot becomes a pilot by learning certain things", "philosophy is like carpentry and piloting in this respect", "in philosophy there is a necessity to learn certain things", "he must try with all his power to be like the gods", "the divine is faithful", "the divine is free", "the divine is beneficent", "the divine is magnanimous", "man must be faithful", "man must be free" and "man must be beneficent" are true, which the second allows
epictetus-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: epictetus-1.logic.r2 has "would_please → (divine_faithful → man_faithful) ∧ (divine_free → man_free) ∧ (divine_beneficent → man_beneficent) ∧ (divine_magnanimous → man_magnanimous)"; epictetus-1.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-1.logic.r2.3 have "¬wish_sufficient ∧ must_learn"; they differ when only "in philosophy it is sufficient to wish to be wise and good", "in philosophy there is a necessity to learn certain things", "a man would please and obey the gods", "the divine is faithful", "the divine is free", "the divine is beneficent", "the divine is magnanimous", "man must be faithful", "man must be free", "man must be beneficent" and "man must be magnanimous" are true, which the first allows
epictetus-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: epictetus-1.logic.r2 puts it at S6, "If the divine is faithful, man also must be faithful; if it is free, man also must be free; if beneficent, man also must be beneficent; if magnanimous, man also must be magnanimous; as being then an imitator of God he must do and say everything consistently with this fact."; epictetus-1.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-1.logic.r2.3 at S2, "May it not then in philosophy also not be sufficient to wish to be wise and good, and that there is also a necessity to learn certain things?"
epictetus-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps epictetus-1.logic.r2.2 and epictetus-1.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S6, "If the divine is faithful, man also must be faithful; if it is free, man also must be free; if beneficent, man also must be beneficent; if magnanimous, man also must be magnanimous; as being then an imitator of God he must do and say everything consistently with this fact.", rather than S2, "May it not then in philosophy also not be sufficient to wish to be wise and good, and that there is also a necessity to learn certain things?"?
